From fe8b506f807854e2f52e287bfc0d4b11e5b9289b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:47:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wow64: PS5: a 32-bit allocation stays below the process's
 limit.

A 32-bit caller's own address requirements (an alignment, say) came
through with no highest address, so the 64-bit side had no limit for
them; wow64 added the process's limit only when there were no
requirements at all. Upstream's search finds low memory first anyway.
The console's allocator prefers its reserved area above 4 GiB: in a
32-bit game 51 aligned allocations (NtAllocateVirtualMemoryEx with one
requirement) landed at 0x10xxxxxxxx, the game kept the low half of the
address, and its first write faulted at 0x43400000 (PS5_Proton
2026-10-07; record removed from the tree; in git history).

No highest address is now the 32-bit process's own limit, as on
Windows.
---
 dlls/wow64/virtual.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/wow64/virtual.c b/dlls/wow64/virtual.c
index b4778a8..ad50ee0 100644
--- a/dlls/wow64/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/wow64/virtual.c
@@ -120,6 +120,10 @@ static NTSTATUS mem_extended_parameters_32to64( MEM_EXTENDED_PARAMETER **ret_par
         req->LowestStartingAddress = ULongToPtr( req32->LowestStartingAddress );
         req->HighestEndingAddress  = ULongToPtr( req32->HighestEndingAddress );
         req->Alignment             = req32->Alignment;
+        /* PS5: no highest address is the 32-bit process's own limit, as on Windows. Upstream's search finds low
+         * memory first anyway; the console's prefers its area above 4 GiB, and a 32-bit game's aligned
+         * allocations landed there, truncated (PS5_Proton, 2026-10-07). */
+        if (!req->HighestEndingAddress && set_limit) req->HighestEndingAddress = (void *)highest_user_address;
     }
     else if (set_limit)
     {
